## 1. The Question for a Small Marketing Agency

**Who controls the AI services we use, and could we continue working if a provider changed its prices, products, ownership or data policies?**

Consider an agency that wants to use AI to prepare first drafts of campaign copy. The owner needs more than a comparison of which chatbot writes the most convincing paragraph.

The decision also concerns the company providing the service, the conditions attached to the subscription, the handling of client information and the agency's ability to move its work elsewhere.

Our recommendation is to treat an important AI provider as a business supplier: understand the dependency before building a workflow around it.

## 2. The Translation: What Geopolitics and Vendor Models Mean for Your Agency

### There is no single measure of AI leadership

The Stanford AI Index 2026 describes different strengths across the AI ecosystem. The United States leads in notable model production and private investment, while China leads in measures including publication volume and patent activity. It also identifies the concentration of advanced AI-chip manufacturing at TSMC as an international supply-chain dependency. [1]

The European Union influences the market through the AI Act, including rules for providers of general-purpose AI models. This is regulatory influence, not proof that European models are technically superior. [8]

**For the agency, the practical conclusion is to assess the specific service and contract rather than choose a tool only because of its country of origin.**

### Separate the product, the provider and the infrastructure

When assessing a tool, record:

- **Product:** the application and subscription plan employees will use.
- **Provider and control:** the company supplying it and any parent or controlling organisation.
- **Infrastructure and data handling:** who processes the information, where, and under which contractual arrangements.

These are not necessarily the same thing. For example, Mistral's commercial terms distinguish between its own infrastructure, partner infrastructure and customer infrastructure. OpenAI also describes regional data controls that are available only to eligible products and customers. [4][5]

### Examples of providers to investigate

These are examples, not a ranking or a claim that the case company uses them.

| Product or service | Provider and corporate relationship | What the owner should check |
|---|---|---|
| ChatGPT Business | OpenAI. Its published structure states that the OpenAI Foundation controls OpenAI Group PBC. Microsoft is an investor, not the sole owner. [2] | The exact business plan, permitted data, retention, available regional controls and how work will be retained outside the chat. |
| Gemini in Google Workspace | Google, within the Alphabet group. Alphabet lists Gemini for Google Workspace within its Google Cloud business. [3] | Whether the account has the relevant Workspace protections, which files Gemini can access and which settings the administrator controls. |
| Mistral Vibe, formerly Le Chat | Mistral AI, identified in its commercial terms as a French company. The sources reviewed here establish the supplier, but do not establish its complete current shareholder-control structure. [4] | The contracting entity, deployment arrangement, business terms and any ownership information needed for supplier approval. |

A supplier's headquarters alone does not establish where every part of the service processes data. Check the actual service configuration and agreement.

### What happens if the arrangement changes?

The following are planning scenarios, not predictions that these events will happen.

| Possible change | Potential effect on the agency | Recommended response |
|---|---|---|
| Subscription prices or usage limits change | The cost of producing campaign drafts increases. | Review total costs, including staff review time, and compare the approved alternative. |
| A model changes or a feature disappears | Existing prompts no longer produce acceptable work. | Retest the workflow using saved examples before relying on the changed service. |
| Ownership, contractual terms or data-processing arrangements change | The original supplier assessment may no longer be sufficient. | Review what actually changed before continuing to send sensitive information. |
| The service becomes unavailable | Staff cannot access the main drafting tool. | Continue from saved briefs and templates using a tested alternative or a manual process. |

Keeping independent copies is particularly important. For example, Mistral's commercial terms warn that customers may be unable to export their data after account termination. [4]

**The goal is not to eliminate every dependency. It is to prevent one supplier from becoming a point where the agency's work stops completely.**

## 3. Illustrative Use Case — Campaign Copy Through CRISP-DM

**Scenario:** A fictional 20-person agency wants to test whether AI can help prepare social-media campaign drafts without making the agency dependent on one provider.

This is a proposed pilot. No time savings, quality improvements or company results have been measured.

| CRISP-DM phase | Application to the agency |
|---|---|
| **1. Business understanding** | Test whether AI reduces the total time needed to prepare acceptable campaign drafts, including human checking and editing. Also test whether another workflow can complete the task if the main provider is unavailable. |
| **2. Data understanding** | Use ten fictional briefs, agency-created product facts and an example brand guide. Exclude client records, personal information and confidential company material. |
| **3. Data preparation** | Put each brief into the same structure: audience, objective, approved facts, tone, channel and prohibited claims. Save these files independently of the AI tool. |
| **4. Modelling** | Use an approved no-code AI assistant to produce draft captions. Here, “modelling” means applying an existing model through prompts; the agency is not training a new model. Test the same task with a second provider. |
| **5. Evaluation** | Record total completion time, factual errors and editing effort. A human reviewer scores brand fit and usefulness using the same checklist. Check whether the alternative workflow can reproduce an acceptable result from the saved files. |
| **6. Deployment** | Start with a limited pilot and a named reviewer. Keep approved prompts, source facts and final copy in agency-controlled storage. Reassess after important changes to the provider or workflow. |

**Proposed decision rule:** Adopt the workflow only if it saves time after checking and editing, meets the agreed quality standard, and can be continued without the primary provider. Remove or correct unsupported claims before any content is approved.

Ten briefs would provide an initial practical test, not evidence that every agency or marketing task would achieve the same results.

## 4. Recommended Tooling — No-Code First

Begin with one main tool, a limited test of an alternative and a manual fallback. Do not buy subscriptions for the entire agency before evaluating the workflow.

| Option | Potential role in the pilot | Effort and cost considerations |
|---|---|---|
| **ChatGPT Business** | A centrally managed assistant for preparing and reviewing draft material. OpenAI states that business inputs and outputs are not used to train its models by default. [5] | Review the plan and permissions, prepare instructions and train the pilot users. Check current licence costs before purchasing. |
| **Gemini in an eligible Google Workspace account** | An option for an agency already working in Google documents and files. Google's Workspace privacy documentation describes restrictions on using customer data for model training without permission. [6] | Confirm the account's protections and file-access settings. Include any additional subscription cost and staff-review time in the comparison. |
| **Mistral Vibe Team** | A further no-code candidate from a different provider. Mistral describes a shared workspace with administrative controls. [4] | Review the commercial terms and test the same briefs. Allow time to learn a different interface and adapt instructions. |
| **Existing document editor and shared folder** | Store briefs, approved facts, prompts, final copy and the manual drafting template outside the AI conversation. | Assign responsibility for access, file organisation and maintaining current copies. |

These are candidates to evaluate, not proven recommendations for the selected SME.

**Suggested effort budget:** two staff members, one preparation session and a small batch of test briefs. Record the actual effort rather than assuming that AI will save time.

## 5. Responsible AI and Trust

### Protect client information

For this pilot, use fictional material only.

Before using real personal data, establish the purpose and lawful basis for processing, minimise the information shared and check the relevant contractual responsibilities. Transfers outside the European Economic Area require additional assessment under the GDPR's transfer rules. The EDPB provides guidance specifically for small businesses. [7]

An EU-based supplier is not automatically suitable for every use, and a non-EU supplier is not automatically prohibited. The processing arrangement and applicable safeguards matter. [7]

### Check the specific plan, not only the brand

OpenAI's no-training-by-default commitment applies to its listed business products. Google's documented Workspace protections depend on the service and account arrangement. Do not assume that a personal account has the same conditions as a managed business account. [5][6]

Also distinguish between:

- whether information is used for model training;
- how long information is retained;
- where it is processed;
- who can access it;
- and whether it can be exported or deleted.

A no-training commitment alone does not answer all of these questions.

### Keep people responsible for the output

The proposed pilot requires a human to check facts, brand tone, originality and suitability before anything reaches a client.

Staff should work from approved source material, flag uncertainty and reject unsupported claims. Their role remains creative judgement and final approval, not simply accepting whatever the model produces.

For example, Mistral's terms explicitly warn that outputs may be inaccurate or non-unique and place responsibility for evaluating them on the customer. [4]

### Understand the agency's role under the AI Act

The AI Act distinguishes between different actors and uses. Requirements for a model provider are not automatically identical to the responsibilities of an agency using the model. The Commission's guidance also identifies AI-literacy and transparency requirements. [8]

Before implementation, check which obligations apply to the agency's actual activity and content. A provider's compliance statement is not a substitute for assessing the agency's own use.

## 6. First Five Steps for an Owner

1. **List the dependencies.** Record the AI tools, subscription plans, suppliers and workflows your agency actually uses.

2. **Set the data boundary.** Decide what staff may enter. Keep personal and confidential client information out of unapproved tools.

3. **Save the essentials independently.** Keep briefs, approved facts, useful prompts and final work in agency-controlled storage.

4. **Run a small comparison.** Test the same fictional briefs with the main tool, an alternative and a manual process. Measure time, corrections and quality.

5. **Assign an owner and review triggers.** Name someone to review supplier notices and reassess the workflow after significant pricing, ownership, model or data-policy changes.

**Start with one question: could your team finish tomorrow's campaign if its main AI service were unavailable?**

## 7. Sources and Initial Appraisal

The geopolitical background draws on the research used for Assignment 2. The vendor and privacy sections add official documentation relevant to the agency's decision.

Vendor documents are used as evidence of the supplier's stated structure, features and commitments. They are not independent proof that a product is the safest or most effective option.

### [1] Stanford HAI — The 2026 AI Index Report

[Read the report](https://hai.stanford.edu/ai-index/2026-ai-index-report)

**Appraisal:** A university-led synthesis with documented indicators and supporting sources. Relevant for comparing AI research, investment and infrastructure. Its national-level findings do not establish which tool will perform best for one agency.

### [2] OpenAI — Our Structure

[Read the structure description](https://openai.com/our-structure/)

**Appraisal:** A primary source for OpenAI's stated governance and ownership arrangements. Relevant to identifying who controls the provider. It is the company's own account and should be checked again when supplier decisions are made.

### [3] Alphabet — Corporate Structure and Business Information

[Corporate overview](https://abc.xyz/)  
[Investor information and business segments](https://abc.xyz/investor/faqs-and-general-information/)

**Appraisal:** Official corporate information supporting the relationship between Alphabet, Google and its Workspace business. Appropriate for corporate facts, but not independent evidence of product quality or trustworthiness.

### [4] Mistral AI — Commercial Terms and Product Information

[Commercial terms](https://legal.mistral.ai/terms/commercial-terms-of-service/)  
[Vibe product information](https://mistral.ai/products/vibe/)

**Appraisal:** Primary sources for the supplier's identity, deployment arrangements, product offering and contractual conditions. Commercial terms consulted were effective from 5 August 2026. These sources do not establish the complete current shareholder-control structure; product claims require practical testing.

### [5] OpenAI — Business Data Privacy, Security and Compliance

[Read the business-data documentation](https://openai.com/business-data/)

**Appraisal:** Relevant primary documentation for business-data training policies and available controls. Commitments must be matched to the purchased plan; the page is not an independent security audit or a guarantee of the agency's legal compliance.

### [6] Google — Generative AI in Google Workspace Privacy Hub

[Read the Workspace privacy documentation](https://knowledge.workspace.google.com/admin/generative-ai/generative-ai-in-google-workspace-privacy-hub)

**Appraisal:** Official documentation explaining Workspace customer-data handling and access protections. Relevant for an agency considering an eligible Workspace account. It should not be generalised to every personal Google or Gemini service.

### [7] European Data Protection Board — International Data Transfers

[Read the small-business guidance](https://www.edpb.europa.eu/sme/be-compliant/international-data-transfers_en)

**Appraisal:** Guidance from the EU body bringing together data-protection authorities. Authoritative and relevant to SMEs assessing transfers outside the EEA. It explains the framework but does not approve a particular agency's processing arrangement.

### [8] European Commission — AI Act

[Read the official AI Act overview](https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/regulatory-framework-ai)

**Appraisal:** Official guidance on the Act's scope and implementation. Relevant for identifying responsibilities in the EU market. Specific obligations must be checked against the actual use case and applicable legal text.

